questions.scenario:
You are debugging a production issue in an e-commerce checkout service. During peak traffic, downstream inventory reservations occasionally fail with an `InventoryException` (an unchecked runtime exception). You discover that when this occurs, payment authorizations are still committed to the database despite the presence of the `@Transactional` annotation. Here is the service implementation:

java
@Service
public class OrderFulfillmentService {

    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository orderRepository;
    @Autowired
    private PaymentService paymentService;
    @Autowired
    private InventoryServiceClient inventoryClient;

    public void processOrder(OrderRequest request) {
        executeTransaction(request);
    }

    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    public void executeTransaction(OrderRequest request) {
        paymentService.authorize(request.getPaymentDetails());
        orderRepository.save(new Order(request));
        inventoryClient.reserveInventory(request.getItems());
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where an orchestration saga in an asynchronous event-driven system hangs indefinitely during network partitions. The coordinator orchestrates remote services using `CompletableFuture` across distributed workers using a shared `ThreadPoolExecutor`:

java
public CompletableFuture<Void> dispatchOrderSaga(OrderContext ctx) {
    return CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> inventoryClient.reserve(ctx), customExecutor)
        .thenCompose(v -> CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> paymentClient.charge(ctx), customExecutor))
        .exceptionally(ex -> {
            sagaRollbackHandler.compensate(ctx, ex);
            return null;
        });
}


Under high downstream latency, thread pool saturation occurs. Some compensation actions are never triggered, and the future pipeline neither completes nor recovers, leading to leaked distributed locks.

You are debugging a production issue where an order dispatch service interacts with a legacy backend via an idempotent fallback pattern. When an unexpected downstream failure occurs, the code invokes a compensation method and attempts to log and handle the error inside an `@Around` aspect:

java
@Around("@annotation(CompensateOnFailure)")
public Object handleCompensation(ProceedingJoinPoint joinPoint) throws Throwable {
    try {
        return joinPoint.proceed();
    } catch (RemoteSystemException ex) {
        logger.warn("Downstream failure detected, running fallback");
        return fallbackService.retrieveCachedState(joinPoint.getArgs());
    }
}


In production, when `RemoteSystemException` occurs during a method also annotated with `@Transactional`, the fallback succeeds and returns cached state. However, the calling framework throws `UnexpectedRollbackException: Transaction silently rolled back because it has been marked as rollback-only` when returning the response to the client.
